vplOperations.details:
Reads the style, works out which source-layers it draws, which properties it reads and which features it filters to, then drops everything else. A Shortbread tileset reduced to a style that only ever reads `name` loses every `name_*` translation, and a layer drawn as plain geometry loses all of its properties while keeping its shapes.

The same reduction can be written by hand as `vector_filter_layers`, one `vector_filter_features` per layer, and `vector_filter_properties`. This does it in one pass instead: chaining twenty operations would decode and re-encode every tile twenty times to touch one layer each time.

**What it reads from the style**, for every visible style layer of this tileset:

- its `source-layer` and its `filter`;
- its `minzoom` and `maxzoom`, as the tile zooms MapLibre loads for them: rounded down, no lower than the style source's `minzoom`, and clamped to the lower of the tileset's maximum and the style source's `maxzoom`. MapLibre overzooms, so a style drawing addresses from z17 keeps them in the z14 tiles of a tileset that stops there;
- every property its `filter`, `paint` and `layout` read — through `get`, legacy filters, `{token}` strings and legacy `{"property": …}` functions — at the zooms it is drawn at, and the source's `promoteId` at every zoom.

A `ref` layer counts with what it takes from the layer it names, as MapLibre resolves it. Hidden style layers (`"visibility": "none"`) count for nothing: a map that shows one at runtime needs a style with it visible for the reduction.

**Which tileset.** Only the style layers drawing this tileset count. A style that draws a single vector tileset uses that one; style sources with the same `url` or `tiles` are one tileset, and naming one names all of them. A style drawing several needs `source`, because nothing in it says which of them this tileset is.

**The reduction may keep features the style never draws; it never drops one it does.** Anything in the style that cannot be read widens toward keeping:

- a filter it cannot read keeps every feature in that layer's zoom range — `match`, `case`, `coalesce`, the geometry type (`$type`, `["geometry-type"]`), the feature ID, `zoom`, string operations, and a modern `in` that tests a substring;
- a property read by a computed name (`["get", ["concat", …]]`) or through `["properties"]` keeps every property of that source-layer;
- a feature that passes the filter but draws nothing, such as an icon that resolves to none, is kept, and so is every geometry type, whichever the layer type draws;
- a property is kept on every feature of its source-layer, not only on the features of the style layer that reads it, and `to-boolean` keeps `""`, `0` and `false`.

What would reduce the tileset to nothing is refused instead: a style that draws none of the tileset's layers, several tilesets without `source`, or only hidden layers.
